<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neo-Assyrian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neo-Assyrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> was the final and greatest phase of Assyrian power, beginning with a revival after 911 BCE and ending with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a> in 612 BCE. It built a tribute-extraction system based on conquest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportation</a> and provincial administration, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 671 BCE. Its wars against the Kushite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a> out of Egypt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire.jpg" alt="Neo-Assyrian Empire" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Assyria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> began with the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-ii">Adad-nirari II</a> in 911 BCE. Its early kings set out to reverse a long decline and retake lands lost in the late 11th century BCE. They presented the reconquest as a war to liberate Assyrians cut off from Assyrian territory. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> (r. 883 to 859 BCE) Assyria again dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>, his campaigns reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assur">Assur</a> to Kalhu, later called Calah in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nimrud">Nimrud</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>. The empire grew further under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a> (r. 859 to 824 BCE).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Shalmaneser's death the empire stagnated in what is called the age of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>, when generals and officials held political power and central control was weak. Tiglath-Pileser III (r. 745 to 727 BCE) reasserted royal power, reorganised the army and more than doubled the empire, notably by conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> and much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. Sargon II (r. 722 to 705 BCE), probably a usurper, founded the Sargonid dynasty. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> (r. 705 to 681 BCE) the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a> the empire reached its largest extent with the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> died in 631 BCE his son Aššur-etil-ilāni inherited the throne against considerable opposition, helped by the chief eunuch Sîn-šumu-līšir. The fall in the late 7th century was swift and violent, brought about by a Babylonian uprising and an invasion by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, and scholars still debate its causes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The empire's success rested on incorporating conquered lands into its administration, which was divided into provinces. Its army made large-scale use of cavalry and new siege techniques, and messages from high officials, stamped with their seals, travelled by relay stations and well-kept roads at a speed not surpassed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> until the 19th century. A resettlement policy moved part of the population of conquered lands to the heartland and underdeveloped provinces, which diluted local identities and helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> become the regional common language until the 14th century. The highest offices in the administration and army usually went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> of obscure origins, which ensured their loyalty to the king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neo-Assyrian Empire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Assyrian economy changed fundamentally over a history of some fourteen centuries, from the commercial network of the Old Assyrian period to the tribute system of the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Kassite period the sources fall almost silent for several centuries, and Dilmun re-emerges in the eighth century BCE as a minor kingdom paying tribute to the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b>, which valued its position but did not occupy it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Israel’s last decades were shaped by the expansion of the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> under Tiglath-pileser III (r. 745–727 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 8th century BCE the kingdom at Gordium expanded into an empire dominating most of central and western Anatolia, bordering the larger and more powerful <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> to the south-east and Urartu to the north-east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Urartu was the most serious and lasting rival the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b> faced in the north, and Assyrian texts, including Sargon II’s long letter to the god Ashur describing the campaign of 714 BCE, are a major source for its history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the seventh century BCE, however, the empire faced the <b>Neo-Assyrian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
